diagnosis_text:
A thorough examination of every layer of the dc_clos_bgp topology found no active fault. All eight eBGP sessions (two leaf-to-spine pairs for each of the two spines, plus two spine-to-super-spine sessions) have been continuously established since lab startup with zero session drops, zero BGP Notifications, and zero interface errors. Routing tables on all five FRR devices are correctly populated: leaf_router_0_0 installs 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1 installs 10.0.0.0/24 via spine_router_0_0. Bidirectional ICMP between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) yields 0% loss, and a traceroute confirms the expected four-hop path through spine_router_0_0. No iptables rules, no tc/netem packet-loss injection, and no FRR process restarts were found anywhere in the topology. The network is currently operating correctly. One noteworthy design observation: neither leaf router has `maximum-paths ebgp 2` configured, so all inter-leaf traffic is pinned to spine_router_0_0 with spine_router_0_1 acting only as a cold standby; a brief crash of spine_router_0_0's BGP process would cause seconds of blackout before failover. However, there is no evidence that such an event has occurred, and the current network state is fully healthy. The helpdesk report of intermittent connectivity does not correspond to any detectable fault — this ticket is a false premise.
=== END ===